## Original Content
Investors will buy your life insurance policy today — and collect the payout when you die.

## Summary
The article examines how the AIDS epidemic contributed to the emergence of a financial market where investors purchase life insurance policies from individuals to profit from the eventual death benefits. This market has evolved into a multibillion-dollar industry, highlighting the intersection of public health crises and speculative finance.

## Key Points
- The AIDS crisis was a catalyst for creating a death-specification market in life insurance.
- Investors buy life insurance policies upfront and collect payouts when the policyholder dies.
- This market is valued in the billions of dollars, indicating significant financial scale.
- It raises questions about the ethical and economic implications of health crises in financial systems.


